const OMGS_SEARCH = [{"page": "Equipment Guide", "head": "", "url": "equipment.html", "text": "Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball Equipment Guide New to OMGS? This quick guide helps first-time families pick a bat and glove that fit. The charts below are starting points, not rules. The best way to know is to have your player try gear on. Get fitted at Scheels. For hands-on help, visit Scheels Sandy (opens in a new tab) . Their associates will help size a bat and glove and get your family set up. Bat length by age Use this chart as a starting point. Many stores also use height and weight charts, which work the same way. Starting bat length by age Age Bat length 5 to 7 24 to 26 in. 8 to 9 26 to 28 in. 10 28 to 29 in. 11 to 12 30 to 31 in. 13 to 14 31 to 32 in. 15 to 16 32 to 33 in. 17 and up About 33 to 34 in. Checking bat length and weight Wingspan check: Have your player hold her arms straight out to the sides. Measure from the center of her chest to her fingertips. That number in inches is another good starting length. Weight check: Have her hold the bat straight out to the side with her top hand for about 20 seconds. If the bat shakes or drops, it is too heavy. Control comes first. A bat she can swing with control is better than a longer or heavier one. For beginners, a little light is better than too heavy. Softball, not baseball. Choose a bat marked for fastpitch softball. Baseball bats are built differently. Certification. When league rules require it, bats must carry the approved certification marks (such as USA Softball). Check your division rules or ask your coach if you are unsure. Batting side: left or right Right-handed batter: stands in the batter's box on the left side of home plate (as seen from behind the plate looking toward the pitcher). Her left hand is on the bottom of the bat and her right hand is on top. Left-handed batter: the opposite. She stands on the right side of the plate with her right hand on the bottom. Many girls throw right and bat right. Some throw right and bat left. If your player is not sure, let her try both. What feels comfortable and lets her see the pitcher well matters most. Please don't force a side. Glove: throwing hand decides Batting side and glove hand are two different decisions. The glove follows the hand she throws with. Throws right: buy a Right Hand Throw (RHT) glove. It is worn on the left hand. Throws left: buy a Left Hand Throw (LHT) glove. It is worn on the right hand. Glove size Starting glove sizes for youth fastpitch Player Typical size Tee ball About 8.5 to 10 in. Younger youth, infield About 10.5 to 11 in. Younger youth, outfield About 11 to 11.5 in. Older youth and adult, infield About 11.5 to 12 in. Older youth and adult, outfield or first base Larger than infield Snug fit. The glove should fit snugly while she can still move her wrist freely. Adjustable wrist straps help. Don't buy too big to grow into. A glove that is too large makes catching harder. One glove for every spot. Young players usually need a multi-position glove, not a specialized mitt. Softball glove if you can. Softball gloves have a deeper pocket for the bigger ball. Other basics Helmet: a batting helmet that fits snugly, with the chin strap fastened. Cleats: shoes made for dirt and grass. Soft or molded cleats are a safe choice for young players; check your division rules before buying metal cleats. Bag: optional, but handy for keeping gear together. Catcher's gear: this is specialized. If your player will catch, ask for help fitting it. Ready to shop? These charts are guides only. Stop by Scheels Sandy (opens in a new tab) and ask an associate to size a bat and glove with your daughter. They will help you get set up for the season."}, {"page": "Home", "head": "", "url": "index.html", "text": "Learn the Game. Play It. Love It. Recreational girls softball for 500+ girls ages 3 to 18 each season. We teach the fundamentals in a safe, fun setting and help girls build skills, sportsmanship, and confidence. Register Find my division Registration is on PlayMetrics. You will sign in or create an account first. Building Skills Building Character Building Champions Building Skills Building Character Building Champions Building Skills Building Character Building Champions Building Skills Building Character Building Champions At a glance"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "What is Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball?", "url": "index.html", "text": "What is Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball? Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball (OMGS) is a recreational girls softball league based in Riverton, Utah. It is for girls ages 3 to 18. Divisions: T-Ball/6U, 8U, 10U, 12U, 14U, and 18U, based on birth month and year. See the age chart and division finder . Seasons: Spring (generally March through May), summer All-Stars (June 1 to early August), and Fall (generally August through October). Every team is guaranteed 10 games each season. Fields: Centennial Park in Riverton is the home field. Teams also play at Tuscany East in Herriman. See fields and directions . How to register: use any Register button on this site. It opens PlayMetrics, where you sign in or create an account. Spring registration usually opens in mid-January. Contact: . What's next"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Four waysto get in the game.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Four ways to get in the game. 01"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Register", "url": "index.html", "text": "Register Spring registration usually opens in mid-January. The 2027 date is not set yet. Every Register button goes straight to sign-up. Register now 02"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Schedule", "url": "index.html", "text": "Schedule Season dates, drafts, meetings, and All-Star events. Game schedules are coming soon. View dates 03"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Standings", "url": "index.html", "text": "Standings Tournament seeding is by league win/loss record. Standings are coming soon. Tournament rules 04"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "All-Stars", "url": "index.html", "text": "All-Stars Summer All-Star teams for 8U through 18U, with the OMGS Invitational and D1 State Tournament. 2027 dates are coming soon. All-Star info Our values"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Play like a GIRL.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Play like a GIRL. G Grit I Integrity R Respect L Love for the Game The OMGS standard"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Building Skills.Building Character.Building Champions.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Building Skills. Building Character. Building Champions. 01 · SKILLS"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Taught right, from the ground up.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Taught right, from the ground up. Fundamentals in a safe and enjoyable setting, with a free coaches clinic, free preseason pitching clinics, and players clinics ($20 for OMGS players, $30 for players not registered with OMGS, in 2026). 02 · CHARACTER"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Teammates first. Always.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Teammates first. Always. Good sportsmanship, positive cheers, and zero tolerance for abusive behavior from anyone at the park. 03 · COMMUNITY"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "A whole park behind every girl.", "url": "index.html", "text": "A whole park behind every girl. Players from 20+ cities across Salt Lake and Utah County, plus volunteer coaches, team parents, BBQ shifts, and clean-up days. Fun, growth, and friendships that last all season. Three seasons"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Spring. Summer. Fall.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Spring. Summer. Fall. Spring"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Spring season", "url": "index.html", "text": "Spring season Our main league season, generally March through May. Dates may change each season. Registration usually opens in mid-January. Players attend a skill assessment (tryout) before draft day, then teams are drafted. Coach picks do not need to attend. Summer"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "All-Stars", "url": "index.html", "text": "All-Stars June 1 to early August, for selected 8U to 18U players. All-Stars have their own tryout and draft, separate from spring. Fall"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Fall season", "url": "index.html", "text": "Fall season Generally August through October. Fall teams: each coach may freeze up to six players in total, including 2 pitchers and 1 catcher. OMGS then builds the rest of each team, keeping teams as balanced as we can by looking at experience, positions, and more. See important dates &rarr; Recognition Program"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Play Like a GIRL", "url": "index.html", "text": "Play Like a GIRL The Play Like a GIRL Recognition Program celebrates players, teams, and coaches who exemplify the core values of our league: Grit, Integrity, Respect, and Love for the Game. Meet the program Nominate a player, team, or coach (opens in a new tab) Game day ops"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Field status.Important dates.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Field status. Important dates. Field status"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "OMGS fields", "url": "index.html", "text": "OMGS fields Fields & directions Important dates"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Mark your calendar.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Mark your calendar. Full calendar Get involved"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "It takes a whole park.", "url": "index.html", "text": "It takes a whole park."}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Coach", "url": "index.html", "text": "Coach Lead a team. Online application, background check, and Child Safety Training required. Applications open soon."}, {"page": "Home", "head": "BBQ assignments", "url": "index.html", "text": "BBQ assignments BBQ assignments: each team is required to cover one 2-hour BBQ shift during the season. In both Spring and Fall, if a team does not cover its BBQ shift, the team forfeits its next game."}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Umpires", "url": "index.html", "text": "Umpires D1 Prospects handles umpires for 10U and up. Brooke Olsen, our Vice President, handles 8U umpires: ."}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Join the Board", "url": "index.html", "text": "Join the Board We are looking for new board members for Spring. Open seats are posted, and applicants are interviewed by the Board. Join the board"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "We're looking for new board members for Spring.", "url": "index.html", "text": "We're looking for new board members for Spring. Help run the league your daughter plays in. Email us to apply. The Board interviews applicants and fills open seats by vote. Email News"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Latest from OMGS", "url": "index.html", "text": "Latest from OMGS Quick find"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Search the site", "url": "index.html", "text": "Search the site Search Searches rules, FAQ, dates, fields, and more. Proud partners of OMGS Your logo here Become a sponsor Follow OMGS"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Stay in the loop.", "url": "index.html", "text": "Stay in the loop. Game day news, photos, and reminders. Facebook Instagram"}, {"page": "Home", "head": "Ready to play?", "url": "index.html", "text": "Ready to play? Spring registration usually opens in mid-January. Register"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "Our mission", "url": "about.html", "text": "Our mission To provide a quality recreational softball program by teaching the fundamentals of softball in a safe and enjoyable atmosphere. To provide opportunities to improve softball skills and learn the value of good sportsmanship while enhancing self-esteem."}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "Our seasons", "url": "about.html", "text": "Our seasons OMGS runs three seasons each year: the Spring season (generally March through May), All-Stars in the summer (June 1 to early August), and the Fall season (generally August through October)."}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "President", "url": "about.html", "text": "President Adam Lloyd"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "Vice President", "url": "about.html", "text": "Vice President Brooke Olsen"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "Secretary", "url": "about.html", "text": "Secretary Tammy Anderson"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "Treasurer &amp; 18U Coordinator", "url": "about.html", "text": "Treasurer & 18U Coordinator Shannon Capson"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "General Board", "url": "about.html", "text": "General Board Name Role Email Kathy Wilfert Clinics & Equipment Jason Woodhead 14U Coordinator Tabitha Stratford 12U Coordinator Audrey Wood Uniforms and All-Stars Brock Cordingley Website, Schedules, and 8U Coordinator Tasha Mayfield 10U Coordinator Melissa Smith Social Media Board members (Directors) do not have fixed terms. When a seat opens, OMGS posts the opening, interviews applicants, and the Board fills it by vote. Executive officers are elected at the coaches meeting when a position is open. Join the board"}, {"page": "About & Board", "head": "We're looking for new board members for Spring.", "url": "about.html", "text": "We're looking for new board members for Spring. Help run the league your daughter plays in. Email us to apply. The Board interviews applicants and fills open seats by vote. Email"}, {"page": "Divisions & Ages", "head": "Find my division", "url": "divisions.html", "text": "Find my division For the Fall 2026 / Spring 2027 seasons, based on the official age chart. Birth month Month January February March April May June July August September October November December Birth year Year 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 2019 2018 2017 2016 2015 2014 2013 2012 2011 2010 2009 2008 2007 Find division Girls born September to December share an age group with girls born January to August of the next year. Final placement is up to OMGS."}, {"page": "Divisions & Ages", "head": "Fall 2026 / Spring 2027 age chart", "url": "divisions.html", "text": "Fall 2026 / Spring 2027 age chart Find your daughter's birth month and year to see her division. Born Sep to Dec or born Jan to Aug Age Division Sep to Dec 2019 Jan to Aug 2020 6 8U Sep to Dec 2018 Jan to Aug 2019 7 8U Sep to Dec 2017 Jan to Aug 2018 8 8U Sep to Dec 2016 Jan to Aug 2017 9 10U Sep to Dec 2015 Jan to Aug 2016 10 10U Sep to Dec 2014 Jan to Aug 2015 11 12U Sep to Dec 2013 Jan to Aug 2014 12 12U Sep to Dec 2012 Jan to Aug 2013 13 14U Sep to Dec 2011 Jan to Aug 2012 14 14U Sep to Dec 2010 Jan to Aug 2011 15 18U Sep to Dec 2009 Jan to Aug 2010 16 18U Sep to Dec 2008 Jan to Aug 2009 17 18U Sep to Dec 2007 Jan to Aug 2008 18 18U View the original age chart image"}, {"page": "Divisions & Ages", "head": "Division basics", "url": "divisions.html", "text": "Division basics Tap a division for its full rules. Division Ball Pitching distance Bases Who pitches Run limit per half-inning No new inning after T-Ball/6U 11\" safety ball Coach pitch, then tee 55 ft Coach pitches up to 5, then tee (3 swings). No score kept. Whole lineup bats 60 min or 3 innings 8U 11\" 35 ft 60 ft Coach pitch 5 runs 55 min 10U 11\" 35 ft 60 ft Player pitch 5 runs 55 min 12U 12\" 40 ft 60 ft Player pitch 7 runs 55 min 14U 12\" 43 ft 60 ft Player pitch 7 runs 65 min 18U 12\" 43 ft 60 ft Player pitch 7 runs 65 min Divisions play in the Spring and Fall seasons. Spring teams are drafted after a skill assessment. Fall teams: each coach may freeze up to six players in total, including 2 pitchers and 1 catcher. OMGS then builds the rest of each team, keeping teams as balanced as we can by looking at experience, positions, and more. Summer All-Stars are for 8U through 18U and have their own tryout and draft. T-Ball/6U: Girls allowed to play in this age group must be three (3) years old through five (5) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year."}, {"page": "Divisions & Ages", "head": "Playing up", "url": "divisions.html", "text": "Playing up \"Playing up\" is on a case-by-case basis, reviewed by the board, however these conditions must be met in order to be allowed to play up. The player must be in their last year of an age group (Ex. second year of 10U asking to play in 12U) and must have played All-Stars in that age group, a parent must volunteer to be a coach, and the player must be listed as a coach pick. Also, being allowed to play up means that that player must try out for the age group they are placed in for all-stars, they may not play back down. The player may be granted to play up to one age group but will then have to request to play up again the next season and continue to meet the conditions to play up. See the rules for details."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "", "url": "rules.html", "text": "Questions? Email us."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "2026 Coaches Handbook (PDF)", "url": "rules.html", "text": "2026 Coaches Handbook (PDF) Full league and division rules, policies, clinics, All-Stars, and safety."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "2026 Coaches Selection (Draft) Rules (PDF)", "url": "rules.html", "text": "2026 Coaches Selection (Draft) Rules (PDF) How coach picks and the draft work."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "2027 D1 Prospects Rules (PDF)", "url": "rules.html", "text": "2027 D1 Prospects Rules (PDF) OMGS is affiliated with D1 Prospects. NFHS rules apply, with OMGS rules taking precedence."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "NFHS Softball Rules excerpt (PDF)", "url": "rules.html", "text": "NFHS Softball Rules excerpt (PDF) NFHS rules apply to OMGS games, with OMGS rules taking precedence. OMGS is affiliated with D1 Prospects."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "OMGS General Rules", "url": "rules.html", "text": "OMGS General Rules Coaches must have their player listed as a coach pick in order to be selected as a coach. \"Playing up\" is on a case-by-case basis, reviewed by the board, however these conditions must be met in order to be allowed to play up. The player must be in their last year of an age group (Ex. second year of 10U asking to play in 12U) and must have played All-Stars in that age group, a parent must volunteer to be a coach, and the player must be listed as a coach pick. Also, being allowed to play up means that that player must try out for the age group they are placed in for all-stars, they may not play back down. The player may be granted to play up to one age group but will then have to request to play up again the next season and continue to meet the conditions to play up. Coaches are required to read the NFHS Softball rulebook, which is linked above, and read and understand the general OMGS rules and special OMGS rules for their particular age group. The NFHS Softball rulebook will govern OMGS games. Only OMGS rules will take precedence over NFHS Softball rules. GENERAL OMGS RULES: Umpires will check that all coaches on the fields are compliant with OMGS requirements. Game time is forfeit time. There will be ten defensive players. No player can sit out for two (2) consecutive innings. There is open substitution. Teams bat all team members present. Regular season games can end in a tie. If there is less than 5 min. left in a game, no new inning may be started. There must be eight girls present to begin the game (with the exception of T-Ball/6U). If a team plays the game with eight players, an automatic out will be taken for the ninth batter only and not for the missing tenth batter. If a team plays the game with nine players, there is no automatic out. If a player gets injured or sick during a game and is taken out and cannot bat, coaches must inform the umpire. If the team still has nine players without her, then no penalty will be given for her missed at-bats, however, she will not be allowed to rejoin the game. The team must have eight players to finish the game without her or will be required to forfeit. Players may be added to the end of the line up if they arrive after the game has started, but it must be reported to the official Scorekeeper and Umpire or will be considered batting out of order. Players put on the line up must arrive prior to their turn at bat or an out will be taken for that batter each time her name comes up on the official scorebook to bat, until she arrives. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or seven runs (12U, 14U, 18U), and five runs (8U & 10U) by the offensive team. Line-ups must be entered into the Game Changer app at least 10 minutes prior to the game start time, even if the previous game is running late. Coaches must meet the umpire at the plate five minutes prior to game time, or as soon as the previous game is over in the case that games are running late. A player must play in at least 75% of her games to be eligible for All-Stars. T-Ball/6U league players are not eligible to play All-Stars. OMGS has a ZERO tolerance policy for abusive behavior. A coach, player or spectator will not direct a derogatory or abusive remark (including foul language) to another coach, player, spectator or umpire. Actions of this kind will cause the suspension of a coach or player, the ejection of a spectator from the park and/or forfeiture of game if necessary. The ejection will last for the entirety of the day upon which the ejection occurs. If the offense is deemed serious enough, the board maintains the right to consider and vote on additional corrective action. A coach that is ejected from a game will be required to miss the next game if not played on the same day. Positive chants and cheers are encouraged and acceptable. It is not, however, acceptable to chant derogatory chants at the opposing team and especially the pitcher. The home team will be the first team listed on the schedule, take the dugout on the third base line, and bat last. They are also responsible for maintaining the official score in Game Changer. BOTH TEAMS WILL BE RESPONSIBLE FOR CLEANING UP THEIR OWN TRASH IN THEIR DUGOUT AND AROUND THEIR BLEACHERS AFTER EVERY GAME IS COMPLETED. If a player misses three or more consecutive required practices without good reason, the coach will be allowed to bench the player for one full game with the Board’s approval. If she continues to miss practices, this should also be brought to the Board’s attention. No team will be permitted to warm up in the infield prior to the game. Teams may warm up in foul territory only. Leading off is allowed after the ball leaves the pitcher’s hand. Intentional walking IS NOT allowed in OMGS recreational softball. Metal cleats are allowed only for the 14U and 18U age groups, for both OMGS league and All-Star tournaments. Girls wearing metal cleats in other age groups will be warned by the umpire and only allowed to return to the game after replacing the metal cleats with rubber cleats or other closed-toe footwear. Penalties for ejections and suspensions are applied based on the infraction."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "Division rules", "url": "rules.html", "text": "Division rules Tap a division to open it."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "T-BALL/6 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "T-BALL/6 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES Girls allowed to play in this age group must be three (3) years old through five (5) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The eleven-inch safety softball will be used. Game length will be 60 minutes or three innings. Bases will be at 55 feet. Games will start at game time regardless of how many girls are there for each team. No score will be kept. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions after the offensive team’s entire lineup has batted. Players may advance one base on a ball hit, the last batter and base runners will run until all have crossed home plate. All players will play defense each inning. For safety reasons, all players MUST start positioned behind the 35-foot arch line until after the batter has hit the ball. A coach will pitch up to five (5) balls to each batter. If the batter does not hit the ball, then the coach will place the ball on the tee and will be allowed three (3) swings off the tee. There are no walks allowed. Only three (3) coaches will be allowed on the field."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "8 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "8 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES A girl cannot play down an age classification. Girls allowed in this age group must be eight (8) years old or younger prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The eleven-inch ball will be used. NO NEW INNING AFTER 55 MINUTES. The pitcher's plate will be at 35 feet, bases will be at 60 feet. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or five runs by the offensive team. All players will play defense each inning. Six players will be in the infield, placed in standard infield positions, the remaining players will play outfield (grass). The infield is defined as the area on the diamond inside the baselines. For safety reasons, all players MUST start positioned behind the 35-foot arch line until after the batter has hit the ball. The outfield arch for defensive player placement purposes will be on the grass part of the diamond. Umpires' judgment will be used regarding whether the ball is in the infield or outfield for base running and time out situations. One defensive coach will be allowed on the field (in the outfield-on the grass) for instructional purposes only. Defensive coaches are encouraged to stay off the field during the second half of the season or if they are asked to at any time by the Board. If the coach intentionally touches the ball while it is in play or causes a defensive player to move by physical contact, the batter-runner will be awarded the base she is running to plus one additional base. If this happens again, the coach must leave the field. No defensive coach will be allowed on the field during the season-end tournament. A coach will pitch the ball (underhand) to his or her own team. The pitcher/coach must remain in contact with the pitching rubber while pitching. If the pitcher/coach intentionally interferes with a batted ball or a play, the batter-runner will be called out and all runners will return to the base occupied at the time of the pitch. If this happens again the coach must leave the field. If the ball hits the pitcher/coach while they are getting out of the way, the ball is live and playable. The pitcher/coach may only talk to the batters or runners between pitches. (No live ball coaching from the mound.) Talking may occur while the pitcher is waiting for the ball to be thrown back to them from the catcher. There must be no delays in catching the ball from the catcher to allow for coaching from the mound. Use your base coaches to talk to the runner(s) and batter(s). The pitcher/coach or base coach may not physically help the runners to stop or go. Runners who are contacted in this manner will be called out. A batter will receive five pitches. Five un-hit pitches or three strikes will constitute an out. A foul ball on the third strike or fifth pitch will not be an out. If a batter after fouling off pitches lets a pitch go by, then the batter will be out. There is no dropped third strike rule. Walks are not allowed. Batters may advance one base on a ball hit in fair territory in the infield. That batter and any additional base runners may also advance one additional base on any misplayed ball or overthrow (such as a throw or running to protect a base) at the runner’s own risk. After the first defensive play and subsequent advance, runners may not advance further until the next hit ball even if another defensive play or misplayed ball is made. A two base advance will be the maximum bases allowed on a ball that stops or is stopped in the infield and overthrown. The runner cannot advance to home on any overthrow. Runners may advance to home base on only plays as a result of a hit ball. For example, if a runner is on 2nd base and is hit to 3rd base and then the ball is overthrown or misplayed, the runner may not advance home. Players may advance more than one base at their own risk on any ball batted into the outfield until the ball has been thrown to the infield. The infield is designated as the base line between bases. The ball then becomes dead, and the runner or runners are stopped at the base they are going to. Players who must return to a specific base will do so after time is given. If a play is made on a runner after the ball has been returned to the infield and the ball is misplayed, the runner is allowed one base from 1st to 2nd or from 2nd to 3rd. If there is a runner on 3rd who is not allowed to advance home, then the runners may not advance past 3rd base. Only one overthrow/misplayed ball per batted ball allowed. Stealing is not allowed. Runners may not advance on a wild pitch, a passed ball, or throw back to the pitcher. The infield fly rule will be eliminated. NO intentional bunting is allowed."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "10 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "10 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES A girl cannot play down an age classification. Playing up is allowed with board approval. Girls allowed in this group must be nine (9) or ten (10) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The eleven-inch ball will be used. NO NEW INNING AFTER 55 MINUTES. The pitcher's plate will be at 35 feet, bases will be at 60 feet. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or five runs by the offensive team. The outfield arch for defensive player placement purposes will be on the grass part of the diamond. Runners may advance as many bases that they would like at their own risk of being put out on an overthrow. The dropped third strike rule applies all season. Stealing 2nd, 3rd, and home is allowed after the ball leaves the pitcher's hand. Coaches may not physically help the runners to stop or go. Runners who are contacted in this manner will be called out. No coach pitch is allowed; girls will pitch per regular NFHS Softball rules. HIT BATTER RULE: If the pitcher hits two batters in the same inning, the pitcher will be warned. If the pitcher hits three batters in the same inning, it will be up to the coach’s discretion whether to leave the pitcher in or not. After four hit batters in the same inning, the pitcher must be removed from the pitching position for the inning. The removed pitcher will be allowed to return to the pitching position in the next inning. All other NFHS Softball rules will apply."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "12 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "12 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES A girl cannot play down an age classification. Playing up is allowed with board approval. Girls allowed in this age group must be eleven (11) or twelve (12) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The twelve-inch ball will be used. NO NEW INNING AFTER 55 MINUTES. The pitcher's plate will be at 40 feet, bases will be at 60 feet. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or seven runs by the offensive team. The outfield arch for defensive player placement purposes will be on the grass part of the diamond. The dropped third strike rule applies. Stealing is allowed after the ball leaves the pitcher’s hand. HIT BATTER RULE: If the pitcher hits two batters in the same inning, the pitcher will be warned. If the pitcher hits three batters in the same inning, it will be up to the coach’s discretion whether to leave the pitcher in or not. After four hit batters in the same inning, the pitcher must be removed from the pitching position for the inning. The removed pitcher will be allowed to return to the pitching position in the next inning."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "14 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "14 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES A girl cannot play down an age classification. Playing up is allowed with board approval. Girls allowed in this age group must be thirteen (13) or fourteen (14) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The twelve-inch ball will be used. NO NEW INNING AFTER 65 MINUTES. The pitcher's plate will be at 43 feet, bases will be at 60 feet. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or seven runs by the offensive team. The outfield arch for defensive player placement purposes will be on the grass part of the diamond. The dropped third strike rule applies. Stealing is allowed after the ball leaves the pitcher’s hand. HIT BATTER RULE: If the pitcher hits two batters in the same inning, the pitcher will be warned. If the pitcher hits three batters in the same inning, it will be up to the coach’s discretion whether to leave the pitcher in or not. After four hit batters in the same inning, the pitcher must be removed from the pitching position for the inning. The removed pitcher will be allowed to return to the pitching position in the next inning. Metal cleats are allowed only for the 14U and 18U age groups, for both OMGS league and All-Star tournaments."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "18 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES", "url": "rules.html", "text": "18 & UNDER SPECIAL LEAGUE RULES A girl cannot play down an age classification. Playing up is allowed with board approval. Girls allowed in this age group must be fifteen (15) to eighteen (18) years old prior to Sept 1 the previous year. The twelve-inch ball will be used. NO NEW INNING AFTER 65 MINUTES. The pitcher's plate will be at 43 feet, bases will be at 60 feet. The offensive and defensive teams will trade positions upon three outs by the defensive team or seven runs by the offensive team. The outfield arch for defensive player placement purposes will be on the grass part of the diamond. The dropped third strike rule applies. Stealing is allowed after the ball leaves the pitcher’s hand. HIT BATTER RULE: If the pitcher hits two batters in the same inning, the pitcher will be warned. If the pitcher hits three batters in the same inning, it will be up to the coach’s discretion whether to leave the pitcher in or not. After four hit batters in the same inning, the pitcher must be removed from the pitching position for the inning. The removed pitcher will be allowed to return to the pitching position in the next inning. Metal cleats are allowed only for the 14U and 18U age groups, for both OMGS league and All-Star tournaments."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "End of Season Tournament Rules", "url": "rules.html", "text": "End of Season Tournament Rules End of Season Tournament Rules (Fall 2026 is single elimination) 1. Teams are seeded by their league win/loss record, NOT team number. In the event of a tie of wins and losses, the tie breakers will be head to head, runs allowed, run differential, runs scored. 2. All OMGS & D1 Softball rules will be followed during game play just like during regular league play. 3. Complete line-up cards with player’s first and last names & uniform numbers must be submitted to each team’s scorekeeper at least 10 minutes before game time. 4. Home team will be the higher ranked team for winner's bracket. For consolation bracket games, home team will be determined by a coin toss at home plate during the pre-game conference. In the championship game, the undefeated team will be home team. Home team will be determined by coin toss for the “IF” game. 5. Home team will keep the official score in gamechanger. Visiting Team will provide one person to run gamechanger for backup. 6. Game Length: Game time is 60 minutes for 8U, 10U, and 12U games. Game time is 70 min. for 14U and 18U games. No new inning will start after 55 minutes or 65 minutes, respectively. However, if a game is tied after game time has expired; the International Tie Breaker Rule will go into effect for ONE inning after time has expired. IF still tied after tie breaker inning, winner will be determined by amount of put outs made, then amount of hits made. A coin toss may be employed if the score remains tied and it is unsafe to continue playing due to DARKNESS or weather. The first tie breaker inning will start with the last batter from the previous inning at second base. 7. Teams will play 10 players on defense with free substitution (4 outfielders & 6 infielders). Outfielders must be on the grass until the ball crosses the plate. Players must play defense at least every other inning. Teams will bat their whole roster. 8U Exception: All players may play defense (6 infielders and 6+ outfielders). All infielders must stay behind the 35’ safety chalk line until the ball crosses the plate. 8. Game time is forfeit time, unless delayed by late running games. It is your responsibility to know when game times are and check for any changes. 9. All garbage must be cleaned out by the team occupying the dugout before leaving. Please wait for the team leaving the dugout before your team enters. 10. If games are canceled due to weather, they will be rescheduled at the earliest possible time. 11. Protests will be ruled by the umpire and board member on duty. Judgment calls cannot be protested. All decisions are final."}, {"page": "Rules & Handbook", "head": "Safety and conduct", "url": "rules.html", "text": "Safety and conduct SAFETY A First Aid kit and ice are available in the snack shack. The Board of Directors recommends that each coach or team prepare themselves with ice packs and band-aids during times when the snack shack is not open. If any player is bleeding during practice or a game, it must be bandaged up (no visible blood) or she will not be able to continue. BATTING HELMETS: Batting helmets, with masks, must be worn by any girl batting or running bases during a practice, practice game, or game, and cannot be removed until the girl enters the dugout area. CATCHER’S HELMETS: A catcher’s helmet and mask must be worn by any girl to warm up a pitcher. Full catcher’s equipment must be worn by the catcher during any game, including practice games. FACE MASKS: All infielders are required to wear fielders masks during games and practice games. The ball can be hit extremely hard off a bat and cause extreme damage. Facemasks significantly mitigate the chances of serious injury. INSURANCE SECONDARY INSURANCE: OMGS will carry a secondary medical insurance. The insurance will pay after the family’s primary insurance has paid. The insurance will also have liability coverage for the Board Members, Coaches, and Umpires. CLAIMS: Please contact a member of the OMGS board immediately if an injury occurs. CONCUSSION CONCERNS Concussions are becoming more prevalent in all sports. You, as a coach, need to make yourself aware of what a concussion is, what the symptoms are, how to prevent them and what to do if you suspect a player may have a concussion. All players must have a signed concussion form with their player registration to be eligible to play in any game. By completing the coach’s application online, you agreed to the following: You will exemplify good SPORTSMANSHIP by yourself and your team and will follow all rules of D1 Prospects (D1 Prospects/NFHS Rules) and OMGS. You will be a good role model when representing OMGS on and off the field. You will treat all players and parents on any team fairly and with respect and support. You have been informed how OMGS will select all-star coaches and players and agree to adhere to that selection process. You understand that the OMGS Board has the right to terminate you as a coach for failing to do any of the above. You will stand by all decisions made by the OMGS Board of Directors before and during league and all-star tournament play. Coaches will not be allowed to attend practices, games or other team activities under the influence of alcohol and/or drugs. Coaches will not consume alcohol and/or drugs during practices, practice games, games, or any other team activity. Coaches will not be allowed to smoke in the dugout or on the field at practices and during games. ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENTS: OMGS requires that an adult female, 21 years old or older, whether she’s a coach, team mom, or parent, be present at all practices and games. Also, we encourage all coaches NOT to take any player to or from a practice or game. Coaches are responsible to teach girls the basic softball skills of throwing correctly, hitting, base running, fielding, catching, and defensive positioning. More advanced skills may be taught if desired. If the coach does not know how to teach these skills, seek help from the Board. OMGS is providing a coach's clinic on March 3, 2026, which all OMGS head & assistant coaches are required to attend. The Board also strongly suggests that coaches take advantage of the internet, information posted on the OMGS website, and coaching clinics held around the valley. UNIFORMS The league provides a uniform shirt and socks. There will be NO altering of league uniforms in any way. If your whole team wants to do something special with the uniform shirts, prior approval must be given by the OMGS Board. BATTING CAGES & OTHER TEAM ACTIVITIES If a coach chooses to take their team to batting cages or chooses to have a team party or other team activity, it will be at each individual’s own risk. Coaches must notify parents of this and receive the parent or guardian’s permission prior to the event. OMGS does not allow practices to be held at private residences. OMGS insurance will not cover batting cage accidents or accidents at a private residence. PROTESTS Protest determinations will be made according to the NFHS Softball rulebook with only OMGS rules taking precedence. All protests will be submitted strictly by the NFHS Softball rulebook with these exceptions: 1) The written protest must be turned into the Age Group Director of the jurisdiction, and will be kept confidential. 2) The written protest will be accompanied by a $25.00 non-refundable fee. a. Protests on judgment calls will be considered invalid. b. Protests submitted incorrectly will be considered invalid. c. Protests on matters, which do not comply with the rules, will be considered invalid. 3) The protest will be made known to the Executive Committee by letter. 4) The Executive Committee will notify the affected coaches of the decision by letter. 5) The protest fee will go into OMG's general fund. PRACTICES / SCHEDULES / GAMES The OMGS Season is 10 games. Regular season games are to be scheduled, Monday thru Saturday. The 2026 OMGS All-Star season will run from June – August. It is up to you as a coach to contact your team and schedule practices. Practice sessions may begin immediately (after equipment is checked out) and teams have been assigned. OMGS expects coaches to schedule two practices a week (Monday through Saturday constitutes a week) prior to regular season games and at least one practice each week thereafter, unless the team has three scheduled games in the week. Coaches are not allowed to practice at a private residence or any church fields, however, coaches must find their own field (check with local school principals – a fee may need to be paid) and set the time for practices. Any field that you practice on in the City of Riverton must be a softball field or have a backstop. Riverton City is trying to eliminate too many different sports from practicing in the same area. If you are practicing at the Tuscany Field in Herriman, you must have a permit form. Please see Adam Lloyd, OMGS President, for permit forms. FIELD CONDITIONS AND RAIN OUTS: If there is inclement weather, game cancellations will be emailed to the league and will also be sent out to all of the OMGS coaches as soon as possible. It is each coach’s responsibility to check your GroupMe after 4:00 pm on weekdays and before your games on Saturdays. If games are rescheduled, the make-up game day and time will be listed on the website only. Please assume there is a game if there isn’t anything posted or you don’t receive an email. If at any time you feel that your team is playing in unsafe weather or field conditions, please report it to the Board member on duty. TIE GAMES: Games that are tied when time has expired and the inning has been completed will remain a tie. Each team will be given 1/2 game in the standings. EXCEPTION: A coin toss may be used to determine a winner in games where the score is tied and there is no time to complete the game, such as a season-end tournament game, including the championship. SEASON-END TOURNAMENT: A single elimination season-end tournament will be held for each age group (except T-Ball). Teams will be seeded based on their final regular season standings. AWARDS & MEDALS All T Ball, 8U, and 10U players will receive participation medals. Medals will also be awarded to the 1st, 2nd and 3rd place teams in each age group (except T Ball) for Spring regular season record, and rings will be awarded for Spring tournament placement. PLEASE NOTE: All equipment is due at closing ceremonies. If OMGS equipment bags are not returned, Spring team pictures and trophies will be delayed. In both Spring and Fall, if a team does not cover its BBQ shift, the team forfeits its next game. Insurance claims: contact a member of the OMGS board immediately if an injury occurs. Claims must be filed within one week of the accident."}, {"page": "Important Dates", "head": "Three seasons a year", "url": "dates.html", "text": "Three seasons a year Spring"}, {"page": "Important Dates", "head": "Spring season", "url": "dates.html", "text": "Spring season Our main league season, generally March through May. Dates may change each season. Registration usually opens in mid-January. Players attend a skill assessment (tryout) before draft day, then teams are drafted. Coach picks do not need to attend. Summer"}, {"page": "Important Dates", "head": "All-Stars", "url": "dates.html", "text": "All-Stars June 1 to early August, for selected 8U to 18U players. All-Stars have their own tryout and draft, separate from spring. Fall"}, {"page": "Important Dates", "head": "Fall season", "url": "dates.html", "text": "Fall season Generally August through October. Fall teams: each coach may freeze up to six players in total, including 2 pitchers and 1 catcher. OMGS then builds the rest of each team, keeping teams as balanced as we can by looking at experience, positions, and more. Turn on JavaScript to see the list, or download the PDF below. Use + Add to calendar to save an event to your phone or computer calendar. All dates are subject to change. Download the 2026 Important Dates PDF"}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "", "url": "fields.html", "text": "OMGS plays at three parks: Centennial Park (our home field), and Tuscany East."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Centennial Park", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Centennial Park 13000 South 2700 West, Riverton, UT 84065 Directions (Google Maps) Directions (Apple Maps)"}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Diamonds", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Diamonds Field A, Field B and Field C."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "T-Ball Bowl", "url": "fields.html", "text": "T-Ball Bowl T-Ball plays on a diamond set up in the grass bowl east of the pavilion."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Grass Bowl", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Grass Bowl Northeast, Northwest, Southwest, and Southeast corners. Same address."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Snack Shack", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Snack Shack First aid kit and ice are available at the snack shack."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Tuscany East (Tuscany Park)", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Tuscany East (Tuscany Park) 5469 W Toscana Way (12745 South), Herriman, UT 84096 Directions (Google Maps) Directions (Apple Maps)"}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Field Status", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Field Status If games are canceled for weather, they are rescheduled at the earliest possible time."}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Spring Preseason Practice Slots", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Spring Preseason Practice Slots Before spring games start, coaches reserve one-hour practice slots. Slots start Monday to Thursday at 4:30, 5:30 and 6:30 PM, Friday from 4:30 to 7:30 PM, and Saturday from 9 AM to 2 PM. Time Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat 9:00 AM Open 10:00 AM Open 11:00 AM Open 12:00 PM Open 1:00 PM Open 4:30 PM Open Open Open Open Open 5:30 PM Open Open Open Open Open 6:30 PM Open Open Open Open Open"}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "Practice locations", "url": "fields.html", "text": "Practice locations Centennial Park: Fields A, B and C Centennial Park: Large Grass Bowl (NW, SW, SE and NE) and the T-Ball Bowl Tuscany East (Tuscany Park)"}, {"page": "Fields & Location", "head": "How to sign up", "url": "fields.html", "text": "How to sign up Coaches sign up through the league's Google Sheet, which the board shares with coaches before the season. Practice sign-up sheet No practice on Centennial Fields A, B and C after games start. All other fields are first come, first served after games start. Please be considerate of others. OMGS provides coaches with a permit for all fields. Have it with you any time you practice at Tuscany. For permit forms, contact Adam Lloyd, OMGS President, . Fall: there are no reserved slots. Fields are first come, first served at Tuscany and the Grass Bowl."}, {"page": "Volunteer & Coach", "head": "Coach with OMGS", "url": "volunteer.html", "text": "Coach with OMGS Our coaches are volunteers, and we could not do this without you. Head coaches, assistant coaches, and anyone helping on the field must complete an online coaching application before the first game. The head coach and one assistant must complete a background check release (through D1 Prospects). Head and assistant coaches must be at least 21, unless the Board grants an exception. Coach agreement: by applying, coaches agree to model good sportsmanship, follow D1 Prospects and OMGS rules, treat all players and parents fairly and with respect, follow the All-Star selection process, and stand by Board decisions. Coaches may not attend practices, games, or team activities under the influence of alcohol or drugs, may not use them during any team activity, and may not smoke in the dugout or on the field. OMGS requires an adult female, 21 or older, whether she's a coach, team mom, or parent, to be present at every practice, game, and team activity. All coaches complete an online Child Safety Training class before coaching. Coaches and assistants attend the mandatory coaches meeting and clinic in March and help at the pitching and players clinics. A coach's daughter must be listed as a coach pick for the coach to be selected. Coach applications for Spring 2027 are coming soon. Questions? Email us. Read the 2026 Coaches Handbook · Draft rules"}, {"page": "Volunteer & Coach", "head": "BBQ assignments", "url": "volunteer.html", "text": "BBQ assignments BBQ assignments: each team is required to cover one 2-hour BBQ shift during the season. In both Spring and Fall, if a team does not cover its BBQ shift, the team forfeits its next game."}, {"page": "Volunteer & Coach", "head": "Other ways to help", "url": "volunteer.html", "text": "Other ways to help Team parent Scorekeeper (GameChanger). The parent meeting is highly recommended for scorekeeping training. Centennial Park clean-up day Board service. When a seat opens, OMGS posts it and interviews applicants. Board"}, {"page": "Volunteer & Coach", "head": "Interested in serving on the OMGS board?", "url": "volunteer.html", "text": "Interested in serving on the OMGS board? Applicants must be at least 21. Board Member Application (opens in a new tab) Questions? Email us."}, {"page": "FAQ", "head": "", "url": "faq.html", "text": "Expand all How do I register? Click any Register button on this site. Spring registration usually opens in mid-January. 2027 fees will be posted when registration opens. Is there a late fee? No. OMGS does not charge late fees. The 2027 registration deadline will be posted when registration opens. Where are games played? At Centennial Park in Riverton (our home field), and Tuscany East. Addresses and map links are on the Fields page. What division will my daughter play in? Check the age chart . It is based on birth month and year. When are the seasons? OMGS has three seasons: Spring (generally March through May), All-Stars in the summer (June 1 to early August), and Fall (generally August through October). See Important Dates . Can girls on competitive teams play? Not in Spring or All-Stars. A player who is registered or listed on an accelerated (competitive) roster or team during the same season as OMGS may not play on any OMGS team. This includes teams registered through D1 Prospects, USA Softball of Utah (formerly ASA), and USSSA. This does not apply to the Fall season. How long is a season? Every team is guaranteed 10 games each season. There is also a single elimination season-end tournament as an extra. Is there a draft? In the spring, yes. Players attend a skill assessment (tryout) before draft day (coach picks do not need to), then teams are drafted. All-Stars have their own tryout and draft. In the fall there is no draft. Fall teams: each coach may freeze up to six players in total, including 2 pitchers and 1 catcher. OMGS then builds the rest of each team, keeping teams as balanced as we can by looking at experience, positions, and more. See the draft rules . Can my daughter play up? Playing up is allowed with board approval and is decided case by case. Certain conditions must be met. See Divisions for the requirements. Do parents need to volunteer? BBQ assignments: each team is required to cover one 2-hour BBQ shift during the season. In both Spring and Fall, if a team does not cover its BBQ shift, the team forfeits its next game. Interested in serving on the OMGS board? Applicants must be at least 21. Board Member Application . What about All-Stars? A player must play in at least 75% of her games to be eligible for All-Stars. T-Ball/6U players are not eligible. All-Star eligibility after a sportsmanship issue depends on the infraction. Details are on the All-Stars page. Is financial help available? Yes. No eligible player is turned away only because she cannot pay registration fees. The Executive Board may approve confidential payment arrangements, scholarships, waivers, or other financial help. Email us . Can I get a refund? If you cancel before teams have been sorted, you get a refund of your registration fee minus a $5 cancellation fee per player. If you cancel after teams have been sorted, the registration fee is not refunded. See Registration Policies . Where can teams practice? Practices must be held at a public park. OMGS does not allow practices at private residences. See Registration Policies . What is the concussion policy? Every player must have a signed concussion form with her registration to play in any game. When you register, you agree to follow the D1 Prospects policy on concussions and head injuries, as required by Utah law. See Registration Policies . Who can argue an umpire's call? Only the coach who met the umpire at the plate meeting before the game may argue a call. Penalties for poor sportsmanship depend on the infraction. See Registration Policies . Is there insurance? Yes. OMGS carries secondary medical insurance, which pays after your family's primary insurance. If an injury happens, contact a member of the OMGS board immediately. Claims must be filed within one week of the accident."}, {"page": "Contact", "head": "Email", "url": "contact.html", "text": "Email"}, {"page": "Contact", "head": "Mail", "url": "contact.html", "text": "Mail P.O. Box 385 Riverton, UT 84065-0385 Looking for a specific board member? See About & Board ."}, {"page": "Contact", "head": "Ready to sign up?", "url": "contact.html", "text": "Ready to sign up? Register"}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "", "url": "policies.html", "text": "When you register a player with OMGS, you agree to the policies below. The accident waiver, release of liability, and authorization for medical treatment are signed at registration."}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "Concussion and practice waiver", "url": "policies.html", "text": "Concussion and practice waiver I, (parent/guardian) hereby authorize my daughter, to play for Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball Association for the D1 season. By accepting this authorization, I acknowledge the following: 1) My daughter will be registered with D1 Softball for the league stated above. 2) Pursuant to Utah Health code 26-53-201, I have read, understand, and agree to abide by the D1 Softball of Utah policy on concussions and head injuries."}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "Practices, batting cages, and team activities", "url": "policies.html", "text": "Practices, batting cages, and team activities OMGS PRACTICES, BATTING CAGES & TEAM ACTIVITIES OMGS does not allow practices to be held at private residences. Practices should be held at a public park. OMGS requires an adult female to be present at all practices. If a coach chooses to take their team to batting cages or choose to have a team party or other team activity, it will be at each individual’s own risk. Coaches must notify parents of this and receive the parent or guardian’s permission prior to the event. OMGS insurance will not cover batting cage accidents or accidents at a private residence."}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "Parental agreement", "url": "policies.html", "text": "Parental agreement I hereby recognize and acknowledge that participation in OMGS activities may involve bodily and/or emotional injury to myself and/or my child. I assume all risks and hazards incidental to OMGS activities and give consent and permission for my child to participate in OMGS events. I hereby voluntarily and knowingly release, waive, and discharge Riverton City and its employees, and all sponsors, organizers, board members, officials, coaches, Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball Association and its officers from any and all liability that may result from my child’s participation in the Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball League. I hereby accept responsibility for my actions as well as those actions of my family and agree to abide by the rules of good SPORTSMANSHIP. I accept that we can be asked to leave the park based on our actions and in the event of extremely poor sportsmanship, may even cause my child’s team to forfeit their game. I hereby authorize OMGS, its officers or representatives to act on my behalf in accordance with their best judgment in case of emergency and agree to assume full responsibility for all medical expenses that may arise there from. I understand that OMGS will carry medical and accident insurance for all players, managers, coaches, chaperons, and board members prior to league play. I understand that this is a secondary insurance and claims must be filed within one week of the accident. By signing this agreement, I acknowledge that I have read and understand its contents and disclosures and I agree to its terms. If an injury happens, contact a member of the OMGS board immediately. Claims must be filed within one week of the accident."}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "Sportsmanship requirements", "url": "policies.html", "text": "Sportsmanship requirements I agree to respect all persons participating at OMGS, I understand that my behavior and the behavior of my guests and players is expected to be in line with the sportsmanship policies of OMGS. I understand that OMGS has a zero tolerance for any person yelling, threatening, using foul language, goading, or any other form of verbal abuse to any person on or off the fields. I understand that the only person allowed to argue a call made by an umpire is the coach that met at the plate meeting before the game. I understand that all coaches and team helpers must remain on the fence or in the dugout while team is in the field and only the third base coach, first base coach and pitcher (8U only) may be on field while team is batting. I understand that any issues with sportsmanship will be an automatic disqualification from All-Stars for your player. I understand that any person that is ejected from a game will be suspended from the next two games and the head coach will also be ejected. I understand that the head coach is the one who makes all decisions where my player will play in the field and bat in the lineup. How OMGS applies these rules: Penalties for ejections and suspensions are applied based on the infraction. All-Star eligibility after a sportsmanship issue is decided based on the infraction. The pitcher (8U only) rule applies to 8U tournaments. In regular season 8U games, one coach may stand in the outfield grass."}, {"page": "Registration Policies", "head": "Cancellation fee", "url": "policies.html", "text": "Cancellation fee I understand that if I choose to cancel this registration before teams have been sorted, I am entitled to a refund of my registrations fee minus a $5 cancellation fee per player. I understand that should I choose to cancel this registration after teams have been sorted, I forfeit my registration fee and will not receive a refund. Questions? Email us."}, {"page": "League Family Services", "head": "", "url": "family-services.html", "text": "Many OMGS families run small businesses or offer services, like custom hoodies and team apparel, photography, and 3D printing. This page lists those services so other league families can find them. Submit Your Service (opens in a new tab) Please note: These listings are provided by league families as a courtesy and include only business and contact information. OMGS is not affiliated with any provider listed, and a listing is not an endorsement or recommendation. OMGS does not receive any payment or anything else from listed providers. OMGS is not responsible for any interactions, transactions, products, services or disputes between providers and anyone who contacts them. Please do your own research before hiring anyone. OMGS reserves the right to decline or remove any listing at any time."}, {"page": "League Family Services", "head": "Browse services", "url": "family-services.html", "text": "Browse services All Apparel & Hoodies Photography 3D Printing Other"}, {"page": "League Family Services", "head": "No listings yet. Be the first!", "url": "family-services.html", "text": "No listings yet. Be the first! Do you or your family offer a service other league families might like? Submit it and we will add it here."}, {"page": "League Family Services", "head": "No listings in this category yet.", "url": "family-services.html", "text": "No listings in this category yet. Check back soon, or see all services. (function(){var b=document.querySelectorAll('.fs-chips .chip'),it=document.querySelectorAll('.fs-item'),n=document.querySelector('.fs-none');b.forEach(function(c){c.addEventListener('click',function(){var f=c.getAttribute('data-filter'),v=0;b.forEach(function(x){x.classList.toggle('is-on',x===c);x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===c?'true':'false')});it.forEach(function(i){var s=f==='all'||i.getAttribute('data-cat')===f;i.hidden=!s;if(s)v++});if(n)n.hidden=v>0||!it.length})})})();"}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Last updated: October 18, 2026 This page explains how the Oquirrh Mountain Girls Softball (OMGS) website handles information."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "No accounts on this website", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "No accounts on this website You do not need an account to use this website. We do not ask you to sign in or create a profile here."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "Cookies and analytics", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Cookies and analytics This website uses Google Analytics to understand how visitors use it, for example which pages are visited and general device and location information. Google Analytics sets cookies in your browser to do this. OMGS uses this information only to improve the website, and OMGS does not sell it. You can read how Google uses information from sites that use its services (opens in a new tab) . To stop Google Analytics from collecting your visits, you can install the Google Analytics opt-out browser add-on (opens in a new tab) . If you close the announcement bar at the top of the page, your browser remembers that choice on your own device so the bar stays closed. That choice is not sent to OMGS. The site loads its fonts from Google Fonts, so your browser connects to Google to download them."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "Forms on this website", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Forms on this website The Play Like a GIRL nomination form, the Board Member Application, and the League Family Services submission form are Google Forms. OMGS uses what you submit only for that form's purpose: reviewing nominations, reviewing board applications, or listing a family service."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "League Family Services listings", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "League Family Services listings Listings on the League Family Services page show the business and contact information that providers submit."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "Player registration", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Player registration Player registration is handled by our registration platform, Stack Sports, under its own privacy policy (opens in a new tab) and terms (opens in a new tab) ."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "Children", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Children This website is not directed to children under 13, and OMGS does not knowingly collect personal information online from children under 13. Parents and guardians handle player registration."}, {"page": "Privacy", "head": "Contact", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "Contact Questions about this page? Email us."}, {"page": "Sponsors", "head": "", "url": "sponsors.html", "text": "Thank you to the businesses and partners who support OMGS. Your logo here Support OMGS by downloading the Land O'Frost app! Land O'Frost Youth Sports"}, {"page": "Sponsors", "head": "Become a sponsor", "url": "sponsors.html", "text": "Become a sponsor Help keep softball affordable for local girls. Sponsorship levels are coming soon. Reach out and we will get you the details. Email us about sponsoring"}, {"page": "Sponsors", "head": "Partners &amp; Sister Leagues", "url": "sponsors.html", "text": "Partners & Sister Leagues"}, {"page": "Sponsors", "head": "Partner", "url": "sponsors.html", "text": "Partner D1 Prospects &#8599; (opens in a new tab)"}, {"page": "Sponsors", "head": "Sister leagues", "url": "sponsors.html", "text": "Sister leagues West Valley Fastpitch &#8599; (opens in a new tab) Mt Olympus Fast Pitch &#8599; (opens in a new tab) Vernal Girls Softball &#8599; (opens in a new tab) Taylorsville Fast-pitch Softball League &#8599; (opens in a new tab)"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Recognition Program Play Like a GIRL The Play Like a GIRL Recognition Program celebrates players, teams, and coaches who exemplify the core values of our league: Grit, Integrity, Respect, and Love for the Game. Nominate a Player, Team, or Coach (opens in a new tab) GIRL Media Package (opens in a new tab) G Grit I Integrity R Respect L Love for the Game This program highlights individuals and teams who go beyond wins and stats to demonstrate character, effort, teamwork, and passion for softball."}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Weekly Recognition", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Weekly Recognition Each week during the season, the league recognizes outstanding examples of what it means to Play Like a GIRL: 01"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Players of the Week (Each Division)", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Players of the Week (Each Division) One player from every division is recognized weekly for: Demonstrating grit and perseverance Showing sportsmanship and respect Supporting teammates Improving skills and confidence Playing with joy and passion 02"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Team of the Week (League-Wide)", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Team of the Week (League-Wide) One team across the entire league is recognized weekly for: Recognition depends on the number of submissions received. Positive team culture and teamwork Respect toward opponents and officials Encouragement and inclusivity Competing with grit and integrity 03"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Coach of the Week (League-Wide)", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Coach of the Week (League-Wide) One coach across the league is recognized weekly for: Recognition depends on the number of submissions received. Modeling integrity and respect Prioritizing player development Creating a positive and supportive environment Representing the values of our league"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "How Recipients Are Selected", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "How Recipients Are Selected Players, teams, and coaches may be nominated by coaches, division leaders, and league officials. Selections are reviewed to ensure fairness, diversity across teams, and alignment with the Play Like a GIRL values. Our goal is to recognize effort, character, and growth—at every age and skill level. Nominate a Player, Team, or Coach (opens in a new tab)"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Where Recognition Is Shared", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Where Recognition Is Shared Recipients are featured on: League social media channels League website Special announcements throughout the season Families are encouraged to celebrate and share these moments of recognition!"}, {"page": "Play Like a GIRL", "head": "Why This Program Matters", "url": "playlikeagirl.html", "text": "Why This Program Matters The Play Like a GIRL program is about more than awards. It's about building confident athletes, positive teams, and a supportive softball community where every girl feels seen, valued, and empowered. Because playing like a GIRL means playing with heart, character, and strength."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "2027 All-Star dates: Coming soon . In 2026, coach requests were due in mid-April, nominations in late April, tryouts were in early May, and the All-Star season ran in the summer. The summer All-Star season runs June 1 to early August."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "What is All-Stars?", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "What is All-Stars? After the spring season, OMGS forms All-Star teams in each division from 8U through 18U. All-Star teams practice together and play a summer tournament season (June 1 to early August), including the OMGS All-Star Invitational in Riverton and the D1 Prospects Rec/All-Star State Tournament. There is no All-Star team for T-Ball/6U."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "Who is eligible?", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "Who is eligible? She must play in at least 75% of her league games. Her coach must nominate her (head coaches nominate all eligible, interested players after talking with families about the time commitment). She must attend tryouts, unless cleared by the board ahead of time. She must be able to make practices and the required tournaments. She tries out only in the age group she played in during the season. Girls who played up try out in the group they were placed in. 2027 D1 Prospects All-Star Rules: All-Star players must come from the same rec league, and a player on an All-Star roster may not be on any accelerated (competitive) roster from April 1 until the State Tournament ends. All-Star teams and players may play in no more than 2 accelerated events in that window. Any exceptions must be cleared by the OMGS board before tryouts. Players on an accelerated (competitive) team roster may not play OMGS Spring or All-Stars in the same season. This does not apply to Fall."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "How coaches are selected", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "How coaches are selected Interested coaches submit an All-Star coach request to the board. The board evaluates applicants on how they coach, not just what they know: developing every player, building confidence, treating players fairly, sportsmanship and composure, clear communication, a positive team culture, and leading the whole coaching staff. Board members with a conflict (they, a spouse, or a daughter are involved in that age group's All-Stars) do not take part in that age group's evaluations. Selected coaches must have a coach application on file and a current background check. Read the OMGS All-Star Coach Standards (PDF)"}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "How teams are formed", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "How teams are formed Tryouts are held at Centennial Park and supervised by the board. All-Star coaches may keep (freeze) up to 6 players from their own spring team. In 10U and up, pitchers and catchers are drafted first, with a maximum of 3 pitchers and 2 catchers per team, so teams have balanced pitching and catching. The rest of each roster is filled through a draft in a set order for each division. The board resolves any disputes. All-Star uniforms use the official league colors: black, lavender, silver, and teal, with the OMGS logo."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "Tournaments", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "Tournaments All-Star teams play a set of summer tournaments. In 2026, players committed to four tournaments. The OMGS All-Star Invitational and the D1 State Tournament were required, plus one chosen by the coach. D1 Prospects also requires each team to play at least 2 D1 All-Star qualifying events to be eligible for State. 2026 tournament (for reference) Dates Sandlot Showdown June 12 to 13 Clash of the Titans June 19 to 20 Gerald Wright Classic June 25 to 27 OMGS All-Star Invitational (CR Hamilton Sports Complex, Riverton) July 16 to 18 Unplugged All Nighter July 24 to 25 Untamed July 24 to 25 A League of Their Own July 31 to August 1 D1 Prospects Rec/All-Star State August 7 to 8 2027 tournaments: Coming soon"}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "Cost", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "Cost All-Star fee: $360"}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "Expectations", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "Expectations All-Stars is a real commitment. Spring teams come first until the season ends, and players chosen for All-Stars are expected to make a reasonable effort to attend practices and every required tournament game. A player who doesn't, or who misses a required tournament game (except for unforeseen illness or circumstances, or with approval from the head coach or OMGS), will not be eligible to try out for All-Stars the next season. All-Star eligibility after a sportsmanship issue depends on the infraction. Selected All-Star players are posted on this website after tryouts, by age group, first name, and tryout number."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "2027 D1 Prospects All-Star Rules (PDF)", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "2027 D1 Prospects All-Star Rules (PDF) Tournament and eligibility rules from D1 Prospects for the 2027 season."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "OMGS All-Star Coach Standards (PDF)", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "OMGS All-Star Coach Standards (PDF) What great All-Star coaching looks like."}, {"page": "All-Stars", "head": "2026 Coaches Handbook (PDF)", "url": "allstars.html", "text": "2026 Coaches Handbook (PDF) Includes the 2026 All-Star policy."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "Everything coaches and parents need for the season, in one place. The 2026 slides are posted for reference. 2027 versions are coming soon . Questions? Email us."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "2026 Coaches Handbook", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "2026 Coaches Handbook Rules, policies, clinics, All-Stars, safety, and forms."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "2026 Coaches Meeting slides", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "2026 Coaches Meeting slides From the March coaches meeting and Annual Meeting."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "2026 Parent Meeting slides", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "2026 Parent Meeting slides For team parents, first-time OMGS parents, and scorekeepers."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "2026 Draft (Coaches Selection) Rules", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "2026 Draft (Coaches Selection) Rules How coach picks and the spring draft work."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "NFHS Softball Rules excerpt", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "NFHS Softball Rules excerpt NFHS rules apply to OMGS games, with OMGS rules taking precedence. OMGS is affiliated with D1 Prospects."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "2027 D1 Prospects All-Star Rules", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "2027 D1 Prospects All-Star Rules Tournament and eligibility rules for All-Stars."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "All-Star Coach Standards", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "All-Star Coach Standards The OMGS standard for All-Star coaches. Heads up: season-end tournaments are single elimination in both spring and fall. If an older document says otherwise, single elimination is correct."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "For coaches", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "For coaches Before you coach: complete the online coach application, the background check release, and the online Child Safety Training. Practices: See Spring preseason practice slots . OMGS expects two practices a week before games start and at least one a week after that (unless you have three games that week). Reserve preseason slots through the league sign-up sheet. After games start, fields other than Centennial A, B and C are first come, first served. No practices at private homes or church fields. In Riverton, practice fields must be a softball field or have a backstop. Equipment bag: each team gets 4 batting helmets with face masks, 2 bats, a catcher's helmet with mask plus shin guards and chest protector (except T-Ball), 6 softballs and 4 wiffle balls (6 softballs only for T-Ball), and a batting tee for T-Ball. Sign it out at the start of the season and return everything at the end. In 2026 a bag cost OMGS about $476 per team for 8U to 18U and $279 for T-Ball. Batting cages and team activities: trips to batting cages, team parties, and other team activities are at each family's own risk. Coaches must tell parents and get permission first. OMGS does not allow practices at private residences. GameChanger: download the app, tap +, and import from PlayMetrics so schedules and rosters are correct. Line-ups go in at least 10 minutes before game time. BBQ assignments: each team is required to cover one 2-hour BBQ shift during the season. In both Spring and Fall, if a team does not cover its BBQ shift, the team forfeits its next game. Rainouts: it's each coach's job to check GroupMe after 4:00 PM on weekdays and before games on Saturdays. Cancellations are also emailed to the league. Make-up game days and times are listed on the website. If nothing is posted and you don't get an email, assume there is a game. If you feel the weather or field is unsafe, tell the board member on duty. Awards: T-Ball, 8U, and 10U players receive participation medals."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "Play Like A GIRL program", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "Play Like A GIRL program Grit, Integrity, Respect, and Love for the Game. Each week OMGS recognizes one player per division, one team league-wide, and one coach league-wide for effort, character, sportsmanship, and positive impact, not just wins. Recipients are entered in a raffle for prizes like tickets, league swag, and custom gear."}, {"page": "Coaches & Parents", "head": "For parents", "url": "coaches.html", "text": "For parents Parent meeting: held in March. Mandatory for team parents, first-time OMGS parents, and scorekeepers (scorekeeping training). No children. Team photos: registration includes one free team photo per player. Photos are taken at Centennial Park, and your coach will share your time. Team treats: treat bags are not part of registration. Families take turns bringing post-game treats or buy them at the Snack Shack. Sportsmanship: positive cheers only. OMGS has zero tolerance for abusive behavior toward anyone at the park. Insurance: OMGS carries secondary medical insurance, which pays after your family's insurance. It does not cover batting cage accidents or accidents at a private residence. If an injury happens, contact a member of the OMGS board immediately. Claims must be filed within one week of the accident. All-Stars: see the All-Stars page for eligibility and how it works. Registration policies: read the concussion, sportsmanship, and cancellation policies you agree to at registration. Questions? Email us."}];
